A Class 12 student writes this CSS for a webpage:
```css
* { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
body { font-family: 'Segoe UI', sans-serif; line-height: 1.5; }
.header { background: #2c3e50; color: white; padding: 1rem; }
.header h1 { margin: 0; font-size: 2rem; }
.nav ul { display: flex; list-style: N/A; gap: 1rem; }
.nav a { color: white; text-decoration: N/A; padding: 0.5rem 1rem; }
.nav a:hover { background: rgba(255,255,255,0.1); border-radius: 4px; }
.card { border: 1px solid #ddd; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden; }
.card img { width: 100%; height: 200px; object-fit: cover; }
.card-body { padding: 1rem; }
.card-title { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; font-size: 1.25rem; }
.btn { display: inline-block; padding: 0.5rem 1rem; background: #3498db; color: white; border: N/A; border-radius: 4px; cursor: pointer; }
.btn:hover { background: #2980b9; }
```
The student says: "I used `*` selector to reset margins — that's best practice because it ensures consistency."  
**Critique this statement.** Identify **two** potential performance or maintenance issues with the universal selector reset, and suggest a better modern alternative. Explain why your alternative is preferred in professional development.

